.TH TDUWEB-RUN-AS-PYTHON25 1 "September 24, 2026" "darpa_spillserver 1.3.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tduweb-run-as-python25 \- run a script under Python 2.7 with the 2.6+ builtins removed
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B python2
.B contrib/tduweb/run_as_python25.py
.I SCRIPT
.RI [ ARGS ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B run_as_python25.py
approximates Python 2.5 closely enough to catch what a source scan misses:
code that uses a name which does not exist on 2.5. The case that motivated it
is
.BR bytes ,
which Python 2.7 has as an alias for
.BR str ,
so code using it runs cleanly on a development machine and dies with
.B NameError
on the TDU.
.PP
It deletes
.BR bytes ", " bytearray ", " next ", " bin ", " format " and " memoryview
from
.B __builtin__
and then runs
.I SCRIPT
with
.BR execfile ,
passing it
.I ARGS
as
.BR sys.argv .
The names that are hidden are reported on standard error.
.PP
Parts of the standard library use some of those names internally, so first it
imports and exercises everything the code under test is known to reach
(including
.B bottle
if it is importable), and gives every module already loaded its own copy of the
names. Only code imported afterwards \(em the code under test \(em loses them.
.PP
It must run under Python 2.7.
.SH EXIT STATUS
That of
.IR SCRIPT .
A
.B NameError
for one of the hidden names means the script would fail on the TDU.
.SH EXAMPLES
Run the TDU web server's self-test as it would behave on 2.5:
.PP
.RS
.EX
PYTHONPATH=/path/to/TDUWeb/server TDU_WEB_NO_SERVE=1 \e
    python2 contrib/tduweb/run_as_python25.py contrib/tduweb/selftest.py
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tduweb-check-python25 (1),
.BR tduweb-selftest (1),
.BR tdu_webserver (1)
